      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a5.19.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g5.19.15', 'g5.19.18', 'g5.19.25'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "The Son can do nothing of himself",
          body: "Accused of *making himself* equal with God, Jesus’ answer is not to retreat from the claim but to redefine what it means. He is equal — and precisely for that reason he acts in perfect, unbroken dependence: **“the Son can do nothing of himself, but only what he sees the Father doing.”** This is the opposite of the human grasp for godhood (a self-made greatness, glory taken from one another, v. 44); it is a Son who wills nothing on his own, seeks only the Father’s will (v. 30), and does exactly and only what he sees the Father doing. The paradox is the heart of it: he is one with God *not* by seizing independence but by having none. Love looks like this — the Father shows the Son everything (v. 20) because he loves him, and the Son withholds nothing of himself in return. So the very submission the crowd reads as a lesser status is in fact the proof of the deepest union: to see the Son at work is to watch the Father, and there is no daylight between them.",
        },
      ],
