      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a5.39.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g5.39.3', 'g5.39.9', 'g5.39.18'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "The Scriptures testify of me",
          body: "Here is one of the most searching things Jesus ever says to devout people. His opponents **search the Scriptures** meticulously — they revere them, memorize them, count their letters — believing that *in the texts themselves* they possess eternal life. And they are half right: the Scriptures do give life, but not as an end in themselves. **“It is they that bear witness about me”** — the whole library is a signpost, and a signpost you can study forever while never walking to what it points to. The tragedy of v. 40 follows at once: *“yet you refuse to come to me that you may have life.”* You can master the map and refuse the country; you can love the Bible and miss its subject. Read rightly, every scroll — Moses most of all (v. 46) — leans toward Christ; and to read it any other way, however reverently, is to hold a lamp and turn from the sun. The point is not less Scripture but Scripture that arrives at a person.",
        },
      ],
